The common theorem
------------------

The theorem ``0b940e5a`` is sound. For the two adjacent dihedral subgroups,
their intersection contains the middle involution. Its intersection with
either cyclic rotation subgroup is cyclic of index two. If the common
dihedral intersection has order greater than four, uniqueness of its cyclic
rotation subgroup identifies the two cyclic intersections. This subgroup is
characteristic in each ambient cyclic rotation subgroup and therefore normal
in both adjacent dihedral groups. It is consequently a nontrivial cyclic
normal subgroup of the generated nonabelian simple group, a contradiction.

The exceptional intersection of order four is handled separately. If its
two rotation involutions coincide, that element is central in the generated
group. If they differ, their product is the middle involution. The two
auxiliary subgroups generated by opposite endpoint and rotation involution
commute and together generate the whole group. Simplicity and the trivial
centre contradict the nonidentity opposite endpoint. The intersection is
therefore exactly the middle involution subgroup.

The other two maximal parabolic intersections follow from the Klein four
group generated by the distinct commuting endpoints. Any larger intersection
would place the opposite endpoint in a dihedral subgroup and make the whole
group dihedral. The final singleton and nested-subset cases in the theorem
then give the full intersection property. No adjacent product order or
coprimality assumption is required.

